 ;; WHAT A PIPER VOICE SOUNDS LIKE - the one thing a list of names cannot say.
 ;; A Piper voice is a MODEL, so the only thing that could answer "what does
 ;; Kristin sound like" is the 67-115 MB you have not downloaded yet. These four
 ;; samples are 0.7 MB together and travel apart from the weights, so a voice is
 ;; chosen by ear and downloaded afterwards.
 ;; Every sample reads the SAME sentence: a sample is a comparison, and a
 ;; comparison where each voice reads different words compares the words. (The
 ;; reference clips above differ on purpose - those are cloned, not compared.)
 ;; Ryan is absent and always will be: CC BY-NC-SA binds the DISTRIBUTOR, so his
 ;; sample is spoken on the machine that chose to install him, never shipped.
 ;; Pocket and VibeVoice have packs of their own; an imported voice is a recording
 ;; that is already on the machine, and a machine with the model speaks its sample.
 {:id "voice-samples"
  :install-dir "voice-samples"
  ;; Named for the VOICE, not for the model that spoke it: the sample of
  ;; `piper-en_US-kristin-medium` is what `{:voice {:id "kristin"}}` plays.
  :requires ["kristin.wav" "cori.wav" "john.wav" "ljspeech.wav"]
  :license "LicenseRef-public-domain"
  :attribution
  "Voice samples generated by the Vis project (bin/make-voice-samples) with the public-domain Piper voices of Bryce Beattie (https://brycebeattie.com/files/tts/) and the public-domain LJSpeech voice. Audio a public-domain model speaks carries no licence forward, so these recordings are published under the same terms."
  :source-url "https://github.com/Blockether/vis/releases/tag/assets-pack"
  :is-commercial-ok true
  :is-redistributed true
